Description
{{more citations needed section |date=April 2026}}
The tree grows to a height of up to {{convert|20|m|ft|abbr=on}}. Its bark is thin and grey-brown, smooth when the tree is young but eventually becoming finely scaly. The twigs are slender and slightly zigzag; they are a light reddish-brown. The flowers are up to {{convert|5|cm|sigfig=1|abbr=on}} long, and are grouped in {{convert|30|cm|in|abbr=on|adj=on}} panicles. They appear in spring and early summer, and last for up to two months. They are followed by woody seed pods, about {{convert|5|cm|in|sigfig=1|abbr=on}} in diameter, which contain numerous flat, winged seeds. The blue jacaranda is cultivated for the sake of its large compound leaves, even in areas where it rarely blooms. The leaves are up to {{convert|45|cm|in|abbr=on}} long and bi-pinnately compound, with leaflets little more than {{convert|1|cm|sigfig=1|abbr=on}} long. There is a white form available from nurseries.
